      text: "Here is the full initial file tree for the application:\n/.env.example\n/.gitignore\n/index.html\n/metadata.json\n/package.json\n/src/App.tsx\n/src/index.css\n/src/main.tsx\n/tsconfig.json\n/vite.config.ts\n\nThe following dependencies are installed:\n@google/genai: ^2.4.0\n@tailwindcss/vite: ^4.3.3\n@vitejs/plugin-react: ^6.1.1\ndotenv: ^17.2.3\nexpress: ^4.21.2\nlucide-react: ^0.546.0\nmotion: ^12.23.24\nreact: ^19.0.1\nreact-dom: ^19.0.1\nvite: ^8.3.0\n\nThe following devDependencies are installed:\n@types/express: ^4.17.21\n@types/node: ^22.14.0\n@types/react: ^19.3.0\n@types/react-dom: ^19.3.0\nautoprefixer: ^10.4.21\nesbuild: ^0.25.0\ntailwindcss: ^4.3.3\ntsx: ^4.21.0\ntypescript: ^7.0.2\n\nKey file contents:\n\n--- /.env.example ---\n```\n# GEMINI_API_KEY: Required for Gemini AI API calls.\n# AI Studio automatically injects this at runtime from user secrets.\n# Users configure this via the Secrets panel in the AI Studio UI.\nGEMINI_API_KEY=\"MY_GEMINI_API_KEY\"\n\n# APP_URL: The URL where this applet is hosted.\n# AI Studio automatically injects this at runtime with the Cloud Run service URL.\n# Used for self-referential links, OAuth callbacks, and API endpoints.\nAPP_URL=\"MY_APP_URL\"\n\n```\n\n--- /index.html ---\n```html\n<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n  <head>\n    <meta charset=\"UTF-8\" />\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\" />\n    <title>My Google AI Studio App</title>\n    <meta name=\"description\" content=\"An application built with Google AI Studio.\" />\n    <meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"My Google AI Studio App\" />\n    <meta property=\"og:description\" content=\"An application built with Google AI Studio.\" />\n    <meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"website\" />\n    <meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\" />\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <div id=\"root\"></div>\n    <script type=\"module\" src=\"/src/main.tsx\"></script>\n  </body>\n</html>\n\n\n```\n\n--- /metadata.json ---\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"\",\n  \"description\": \"\",\n  \"requestFramePermissions\": [],\n  \"majorCapabilities\": [\"MAJOR_CAPABILITY_SERVER_SIDE_GEMINI_API\"]\n}\n\n```\n\n--- /src/App.tsx ---\n```typescript\n/**\n * @license\n * SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0\n */\n\nexport default function App() {\n  return <div></div>;\n}\n\n```\n\n--- /src/index.css ---\n```css\n@import \"tailwindcss\";\n\n```\n\n--- /src/main.tsx ---\n```typescript\nimport {createRoot} from \'react-dom/client\';\nimport App from \'./App.tsx\';\nimport \'./index.css\';\n\ncreateRoot(document.getElementById(\'root\')!).render(<App />);\n\n```\n\n--- /tsconfig.json ---\n```json\n{\n  \"compilerOptions\": {\n    \"target\": \"ES2022\",\n    \"experimentalDecorators\": true,\n    \"useDefineForClassFields\": false,\n    \"module\": \"ESNext\",\n    \"types\": [\"vite/client\"],\n    \"lib\": [\n      \"ES2022\",\n      \"DOM\",\n      \"DOM.Iterable\"\n    ],\n    \"skipLibCheck\": true,\n    \"moduleResolution\": \"bundler\",\n    \"isolatedModules\": true,\n    \"moduleDetection\": \"force\",\n    \"allowJs\": true,\n    \"jsx\": \"react-jsx\",\n    \"paths\": {\n      \"@/*\": [\n        \"./*\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"allowImportingTsExtensions\": true,\n    \"noEmit\": true\n  },\n  \"exclude\": [\"dist\"]\n}\n\n```\n\n--- /vite.config.ts ---\n```typescript\nimport tailwindcss from \'@tailwindcss/vite\';\nimport react from \'@vitejs/plugin-react\';\nimport path from \'path\';\nimport {defineConfig} from \'vite\';\n\nexport default defineConfig(() => {\n  return {\n    plugins: [react(), tailwindcss()],\n    resolve: {\n      alias: {\n        \'@\': path.resolve(__dirname, \'.\'),\n      },\n    },\n    server: {\n      // HMR is disabled in AI Studio via DISABLE_HMR env var.\n      // Do not modify\342\200\224file watching is disabled to prevent flickering during agent edits.\n      hmr: process.env.DISABLE_HMR !== \'true\',\n      // Disable file watching when DISABLE_HMR is true to save CPU during agent edits.\n      watch: process.env.DISABLE_HMR === \'true\' ? null : {},\n    },\n  };\n});\n\n```"
    }
    captured_user_requests {
      text: "# FIXMYRAK \342\200\224 COMPLETE AI-POWERED COMMUNITY REPORTING PLATFORM\n\n## ROLE AND OBJECTIVE\n\nAct as a senior full-stack software engineer, professional UI/UX designer, Firebase architect, database engineer and AI integration specialist.\n\nBuild a complete, polished, production-quality web application called **FixMyRAK**.\n\nFixMyRAK is an AI-powered community infrastructure reporting platform designed specifically for Ras Al Khaimah, United Arab Emirates.\n\nThe purpose of the platform is to allow residents and visitors to report public infrastructure problems, view reported issues on an interactive map, monitor report progress and help make their communities better.\n\nThis must be a complete functional application, not a static mockup or frontend demonstration.\n\nBuild the entire application, including:\n- Complete frontend\n- Backend and server-side functionality\n- Firebase integration\n- User authentication\n- Cloud database\n- Image uploads\n- Google Gemini AI integration\n- Interactive maps\n- Admin dashboard\n- Report management\n- Analytics\n- Security and validation\n\nDo not skip important functionality or substitute working features with fake demonstrations.\n\n---\n\n# PART 1 \342\200\224 BRAND IDENTITY AND DESIGN\n\n## 1.1 Brand\n\nApplication name: FixMyRAK\n\nTagline: Your Community. Your Voice. A Better RAK.\n\nLocation: Ras Al Khaimah, UAE.\n\nFixMyRAK should feel like a professional civic technology startup.\n\nThe design should communicate:\n- Trust\n- Community\n- Innovation\n- Accessibility\n- Simplicity\n- Transparency\n\n## 1.2 Visual design\n\nCreate a modern, premium and professional interface.\n\nUse:\n- A consistent colour palette\n- Professional typography\n- Clean spacing\n- Rounded cards\n- Subtle shadows\n- Smooth transitions\n- Attractive icons\n- High-quality photographs\n- Consistent buttons and input fields\n\nUse a professional civic-tech visual identity.\n\nAvoid:\n- Generic AI-generated dashboard appearances\n- Excessive gradients\n- Unnecessary animations\n- Cluttered interfaces\n- Oversized text\n- Random colours\n- Unfinished placeholder sections\n\nThe website should look like a real product that could be launched to the public.\n\n## 1.3 Responsive design\n\nEvery page must work properly on:\n- Desktop computers\n- Laptops\n- Tablets\n- Mobile phones\n\nCreate a fully responsive navigation system with an appropriate mobile menu.\n\n---\n\n# PART 2 \342\200\224 WEBSITE STRUCTURE\n\nBuild the following pages and connect them properly.\n\n## 2.1 Homepage\n\nCreate a visually impressive landing page.\n\nInclude:\n\n1. Navigation bar\n   - FixMyRAK logo\n   - Home\n   - Explore Reports\n   - Report an Issue\n   - About\n   - Sign In / Profile\n\n2. Hero section\n   - Large attractive heading\n   - Short description explaining FixMyRAK\n   - Attractive Ras Al Khaimah imagery\n   - Primary button: Report an Issue\n   - Secondary button: Explore Reports\n\n3. Statistics section\n   - Total reports\n   - Issues being reviewed\n   - Issues in progress\n   - Resolved issues\n\n   These statistics must come from Firebase, not hardcoded values.\n\n4. How it works section\n   - Report a problem\n   - Location and details are recorded\n   - Follow the report\'s progress\n   - View updates\n\n5. Issue categories\n   - Roads and Potholes\n   - Streetlights\n   - Waste Management\n   - Pavements\n   - Public Facilities\n   - Other Infrastructure\n\n6. Recent reports section\n   - Show recent community reports.\n   - Display issue category, location, submission date and status.\n   - Clicking a report should open its details.\n\n7. Map preview\n   - Show reported issues around RAK.\n   - Allow users to explore the map.\n\n8. Footer\n   - About FixMyRAK\n   - Navigation links\n   - Contact information placeholder\n   - Privacy Policy\n   - Terms of Service\n\nDo not invent official partnerships, government endorsements or claims that reports are sent directly to authorities.\n\n---\n\n# PART 3 \342\200\224 USER AUTHENTICATION\n\nUse Firebase Authentication.\n\nImplement:\n\n- Google Sign-In\n- Email and password registration\n- Email and password login\n- Logout\n- Password reset\n- Authentication state persistence\n- Protected account pages\n\nCreate a professional login and registration interface.\n\nUser profiles should contain:\n- Display name\n- Email address\n- Profile photograph where available\n- Account creation date\n- User ID\n\nDo not expose private user information publicly.\n\nUsers should be able to access their own profile and submitted reports.\n\n---\n\n# PART 4 \342\200\224 REPORT SUBMISSION SYSTEM\n\nThis is one of the most important features.\n\nCreate a fully functional report submission system.\n\n## 4.1 Report form\n\nAllow users to submit a report containing:\n\n- Issue title\n- Issue category\n- Detailed description\n- Location\n- Map coordinates\n- Optional photograph\n- Submission date and time\n\nInclude form validation.\n\nUsers should be able to select their location using an interactive map.\n\nAllow users to upload photographs from their device.\n\nShow an image preview before submission.\n\n## 4.2 Report submission process\n\nWhen a user submits a report:\n\n1. Validate the submitted information.\n2. Upload the photograph to Firebase Storage if one is provided.\n3. Save the report to Cloud Firestore.\n4. Generate a unique report ID.\n5. Record the submission timestamp.\n6. Set the initial status to Submitted.\n7. Display a success message.\n8. Make the report visible in the appropriate public areas.\n\nNever show a successful submission message unless the database operation actually succeeds.\n\n## 4.3 Report status\n\nEvery report must have one of these statuses:\n\n- Submitted\n- Under Review\n- In Progress\n- Resolved\n\nUse visually distinct status indicators.\n\nOnly authorised administrators should be able to change report statuses.\n\nKeep a status history containing:\n- Previous status\n- New status\n- Timestamp\n- Administrator who made the change\n- Optional update message\n\n---\n\n# PART 5 \342\200\224 INTERACTIVE MAP\n\nCreate a professional interactive map focused on Ras Al Khaimah.\n\nThe map is a major feature of FixMyRAK.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- Display community-submitted reports as map markers.\n- Use different marker colours for different statuses.\n- Allow users to click markers.\n- Display report information when a marker is selected.\n- Show issue category.\n- Show report description.\n- Show report status.\n- Show submission date.\n- Allow filtering by category.\n- Allow filtering by status.\n- Include map zoom controls.\n- Include location selection when submitting reports.\n\nOnly display reports with valid coordinates.\n\nDo not generate fake community reports.\n\nUse a suitable map provider and keep API credentials secure where required.\n\n---\n\n# PART 6 \342\200\224 REPORT EXPLORER\n\nCreate a dedicated Explore Reports page.\n\nInclude:\n\n- Search bar\n- Category filters\n- Status filters\n- Sorting options\n- Report cards\n- Report photographs\n- Location information\n- Report dates\n- Status labels\n\nUsers should be able to:\n- Search reports.\n- Browse all public reports.\n- Filter by issue category.\n- Filter by status.\n- Open individual report details.\n- View the location on the map.\n\nAdd pagination or efficient loading for larger collections.\n\n---\n\n# PART 7 \342\200\224 USER DASHBOARD\n\nCreate a dashboard for authenticated users.\n\nDisplay:\n\n- Total reports submitted by the user\n- Reports awaiting review\n- Reports in progress\n- Resolved reports\n\nInclude:\n\n- My Reports\n- Report details\n- Status tracking\n- Submission dates\n- Profile settings\n\nUsers should be able to view and manage their own submissions.\n\nAllow report deletion where appropriate, with confirmation before deletion.\n\nDo not allow users to change official report statuses.\n\n---\n\n# PART 8 \342\200\224 ADMIN DASHBOARD\n\nCreate a separate protected administration dashboard.\n\nOnly authorised administrators can access it.\n\nInclude:\n\n## Overview\n- Total reports\n- New reports\n- Reports under review\n- Reports in progress\n- Resolved reports\n- Reports by category\n\n## Report management\nAdministrators should be able to:\n- View submitted reports.\n- Search reports.\n- Filter reports.\n- Open report details.\n- Review photographs.\n- Update report status.\n- Add administrative update messages.\n- Mark reports as verified where appropriate.\n- Flag inappropriate or duplicate submissions.\n\n## Analytics\nDisplay:\n- Reports over time\n- Most common issue categories\n- Report status distribution\n- Geographic distribution of reports\n\nUse actual Firestore data.\n\nDo not create a hidden administrator account or hardcode administrator passwords.\n\nUse secure role-based permissions.\n\n---\n\n# PART 9 \342\200\224 GOOGLE GEMINI AI INTEGRATION\n\nAI is a core part of this project.\n\nUse Google Gemini through Google AI Studio.\n\nBuild a useful AI-powered feature called:\n\n**FixMyRAK AI Assistant**\n\nThe AI should improve the reporting process rather than simply acting as a generic chatbot.\n\n## 9.1 Intelligent issue categorisation\n\nWhen users enter a report description, Gemini should:\n\n1. Read the description.\n2. Understand the reported problem.\n3. Identify the most appropriate category.\n4. Suggest a suitable report title.\n5. Produce a short summary.\n6. Return structured output.\n\nExample:\n\nUser description:\n\"There is a large hole in the road near a residential area.\"\n\nAI suggestion:\n- Category: Roads and Potholes\n- Suggested title: Damaged Road Surface\n- Summary: A road surface defect has been reported in a residential area.\n\nUsers must be able to review and edit AI suggestions before submitting.\n\n## 9.2 AI report improvement\n\nAllow users to ask AI to improve the clarity of their descriptions.\n\nThe AI should:\n- Correct unclear wording.\n- Improve readability.\n- Preserve the original meaning.\n- Avoid inventing facts.\n- Never add details the user did not provide.\n\n## 9.3 Duplicate report assistance\n\nCreate an AI-assisted duplicate detection system.\n\nCompare a new report against existing reports using:\n- Issue category\n- Description similarity\n- Geographic proximity\n- Existing report status\n\nDisplay possible matching reports to the user.\n\nDo not automatically reject reports just because they appear similar.\n\n## 9.4 AI report summarisation\n\nAllow administrators to generate concise summaries of reports.\n\nThe AI should highlight:\n- Main issue\n- Location\n- Important details\n- Possible urgency indicators\n\nAI must not make official decisions or falsely claim that an issue has been resolved.\n\n## 9.5 AI response format\n\nUse structured JSON output for AI categorisation and summaries.\n\nValidate AI responses before using them.\n\nHandle API errors and rate limits gracefully.\n\n## 9.6 AI security\n\nAll Gemini API calls requiring a private API key must happen server-side.\n\nStore the Gemini API key in Google AI Studio\'s secure Secrets configuration.\n\nNever place private Gemini API keys in frontend JavaScript or publicly accessible files.\n\nDo not expose API credentials in browser network responses.\n\n---\n\n# PART 10 \342\200\224 FIREBASE BACKEND\n\nUse Firebase as the main backend infrastructure.\n\nUse Google AI Studio\'s Firebase integration to provision and configure the project.\n\n## 10.1 Firebase Authentication\n\nConfigure Google Sign-In and email/password authentication.\n\n## 10.2 Cloud Firestore\n\nUse Firestore as the primary database.\n\nCreate a well-organised database structure.\n\nSuggested collections:\n\n### users\n- uid\n- displayName\n- email\n- photoURL\n- role\n- createdAt\n\n### reports\n- reportId\n- userId\n- title\n- description\n- category\n- location\n- latitude\n- longitude\n- imageURL\n- status\n- isVerified\n- createdAt\n- updatedAt\n\n### reportUpdates\n- updateId\n- reportId\n- previousStatus\n- newStatus\n- message\n- adminId\n- createdAt\n\n### notifications\n- notificationId\n- userId\n- title\n- message\n- read\n- createdAt\n\n### feedback\n- feedbackId\n- userId\n- rating\n- message\n- createdAt\n\nUse appropriate document relationships and efficient Firestore queries.\n\nUse Firestore timestamps rather than relying on client device time.\n\n## 10.3 Firebase Storage\n\nUse Firebase Storage for report photographs.\n\nRequirements:\n- Validate file types.\n- Set reasonable upload size limits.\n- Restrict uploads to authenticated users.\n- Use secure file paths.\n- Restrict deletion permissions.\n- Prevent unauthorised access.\n\n## 10.4 Security Rules\n\nCreate restrictive Firestore and Storage security rules.\n\nRequirements:\n- Users can only edit their own profile information.\n- Users cannot assign themselves administrator privileges.\n- Users can submit reports only as authenticated users.\n- Users cannot modify administrative status fields.\n- Administrators must be authorised through secure server-controlled role assignments.\n- Users cannot read private information belonging to other users.\n- Public report data must not expose private user information.\n- Validate all important fields.\n- Protect against unauthorised deletion and modification.\n\nNever use unrestricted rules such as allow read, write: if true.\n\nDo not rely on frontend checks alone for security.\n\nReview all generated Firebase security rules before considering the application complete.\n\n---\n\n# PART 11 \342\200\224 NOTIFICATIONS\n\nCreate a notification system.\n\nUsers should receive in-app notifications when:\n- Their report is successfully submitted.\n- Their report status changes.\n- An administrator adds an update.\n- Their report is marked as resolved.\n\nInclude:\n- Notification icon\n- Unread notification count\n- Notification list\n- Mark as read\n- Mark all as read\n\nNotifications must be stored in Firestore.\n\n---\n\n# PART 12 \342\200\224 SEARCH AND FILTERING\n\nImplement global and report-specific search.\n\nAllow users to search by:\n- Report title\n- Description\n- Category\n- Location\n\nInclude category and status filters.\n\nEnsure search results are based on actual database records.\n\n---\n\n# PART 13 \342\200\224 FEEDBACK SYSTEM\n\nCreate a feedback page where users can submit:\n\n- Overall experience rating\n- Suggestions\n- Problems encountered\n- Additional feature requests\n\nStore feedback in Firestore.\n\nAllow administrators to view feedback from the admin dashboard.\n\n---\n\n# PART 14 \342\200\224 ABOUT AND TRANSPARENCY\n\nCreate a professional About page explaining:\n\n- What FixMyRAK is.\n- Why the platform was created.\n- The types of problems it covers.\n- How the reporting system works.\n- How users can track their reports.\n\nClearly explain that community-submitted reports are not automatically official government reports.\n\nDo not claim that FixMyRAK is affiliated with the Ras Al Khaimah government or that authorities receive reports unless an actual authorised integration exists.\n\nInclude a privacy policy and terms of use.\n\n---\n\n# PART 15 \342\200\224 TECHNICAL ARCHITECTURE\n\nUse a modern, maintainable full-stack architecture supported by Google AI Studio.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- Well-organised project structure.\n- Reusable components.\n- Modular code.\n- Type-safe data structures where appropriate.\n- Secure server-side Gemini integration.\n- Firebase SDK integration.\n- Proper error handling.\n- Loading states.\n- Form validation.\n- Efficient database queries.\n- Responsive layouts.\n- Accessible controls.\n\nUse the Node.js server-side runtime for functionality that requires private secrets.\n\nDo not expose sensitive credentials in frontend code.\n\nDo not introduce unnecessary dependencies.\n\nKeep the code readable and maintainable.\n\n---\n\n# PART 16 \342\200\224 TESTING\n\nTest the complete application.\n\nAuthentication:\n- Registration\n- Login\n- Logout\n- Google Sign-In\n- Password reset\n\nReports:\n- Report submission\n- Photograph upload\n- Database saving\n- Report tracking\n- Report deletion permissions\n\nMaps:\n- Marker display\n- Location selection\n- Filters\n- Report details\n\nAI:\n- Categorisation\n- Description improvement\n- Summarisation\n- Duplicate suggestions\n- Error handling\n\nAdmin:\n- Access restrictions\n- Status updates\n- Report management\n- Analytics\n\nSecurity:\n- Unauthorised access\n- Database permissions\n- Storage permissions\n- User role restrictions\n\nDo not claim testing succeeded unless it was actually performed.\n\n---\n\n# PART 17 \342\200\224 IMPLEMENTATION WORKFLOW\n\nWork systematically.\n\nDo not generate a superficial prototype and stop.\n\n### Stage 1: Architecture\nPlan the entire application and database architecture.\n\n### Stage 2: UI\nBuild all pages and reusable interface components.\n\n### Stage 3: Firebase\nProvision Firebase, configure Authentication, Firestore and Storage.\n\n### Stage 4: Core functionality\nImplement reporting, maps, dashboards and report tracking.\n\n### Stage 5: AI\nIntegrate Gemini and implement all AI features.\n\n### Stage 6: Administration\nImplement the protected admin dashboard and management features.\n\n### Stage 7: Security\nImplement and review all Firebase security rules and server-side protections.\n\n### Stage 8: Testing\nTest every feature and fix errors.\n\n### Stage 9: Final polish\nImprove visual consistency, responsiveness, performance and accessibility.\n\nIf Firebase setup requires user confirmation, stop and show the setup card rather than pretending it has been completed.\n\nIf credentials or configuration are required, clearly explain which settings need user action. Never ask the user to paste private service account keys into the chat.\n\n---\n\n# FINAL EXPECTATION\n\nThe finished application should feel like a real, professional AI-powered civic technology startup.\n\nIt must combine:\n\n1. A beautiful, complete website.\n2. A fully working community reporting platform.\n3. Firebase Authentication and Firestore.\n4. Secure image uploads.\n5. An interactive map.\n6. User dashboards.\n7. An administration dashboard.\n8. Google Gemini AI functionality.\n9. Report tracking and notifications.\n10. Proper security and privacy.\n\nPrioritise working functionality over unnecessary complexity.\n\nDo not remove important features to save time.\n\nDo not use fake data as real information.\n\nDo not leave buttons that do nothing.\n\nBuild FixMyRAK as a cohesive, complete product, not a collection of disconnected pages. Ensure seamless navigation and responsive performance across all mobile devices."
    }
    captured_user_requests {
      text: "Firebase terms accepted, continue setting up database.\n[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_START]\nuser_confirmed_in_ui: true\n[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_END]"
    }
