  view_file {
    absolute_path_uri: "file:///skills/system_skills/firebase-integration-rpc/SKILL.md"
    end_line: 99
    content: "---\nname: \"firebase-integration-rpc\"\ndescription: |\n  Integrates Firebase (Firestore and Auth) into applications, generate secure rules, and handle errors.\n\n  Use this skill whenever the application requires:\n  * Persistent Data & Database: Storing, querying, and persisting application state, documents, or records across sessions and devices (even if Firebase is not explicitly named).\n  * Multi-Role & Access Control (RBAC): Applications with distinct user roles or permissions (e.g., admins, super admins, managers, employees, assignees, creators, customers).\n  * User Authentication & Accounts: App user sign-in/login, user profiles, credentials, or private user data.\n  * Security Hardening & Rules: Designing schemas, writing/auditing `firestore.rules`, enforcing role validation, or debugging permission errors.\n  * SDK & Tool Setup: Provisioning Firebase via `ProvisionFirebase` RPC, initializing the Firebase SDK in React/TypeScript, or deploying rules via `DeployRules` RPC.\n\n  Do NOT use this skill for:\n  * General GCP resource management outside of the specific Firebase tools provided.\n  * Purely transient or single-session apps with no persistence, user accounts, or shared state (e.g., simple unit converters, local calculators).\n  * Applications explicitly requesting a relational SQL database like PostgreSQL or Cloud SQL (use cloudsql-setup instead).\n---\n\n# Firebase Integration Skill\n\nThe Firebase Integration Skill enables the agent to seamlessly incorporate\nFirebase services into applications. It provides a structured approach to data\nmodeling, security rule generation, and error handling, ensuring that\napplications are both functional and secure by default.\n\n## Prerequisites and Tool Usage\n\nBefore proceeding with the detailed instructions, ensure you use the appropriate\nRPC actions when needed:\n\n1.  **Provision Firebase (MANDATORY)**:\n\n    Before writing ANY Firebase integration code, you MUST configure Firebase.\n    The Firebase setup process always follows these steps:\n\n    1.  Call `show_aistudio_ui` with `ui_type: \"firebase_setup\"`. *Immediately\n        after calling `show_aistudio_ui`, you MUST STOP the conversation and\n        wait for the user to respond.*\n    2.  When you are resumed after the user interacts with the UI:\n        *   If the user declined Firebase setup, do NOT call any Firebase RPCs\n            or implement Firebase!\n        *   Otherwise, call `rpc_action` with `service_name: \"fax\"`,\n            `method_name: \"GetFirebaseTos\"` to verify that the terms have been\n            accepted. Arguments: None.\n    3.  **Critical Resuming Behavior**: Treat the terms as accepted ONLY if the\n        `GetFirebaseTos` result explicitly contains `\"accepted\": true`. An empty\n        result (`{}`), a result without an `accepted` field, or `\"accepted\":\n        false` all mean the terms have NOT been accepted; never infer acceptance\n        from the absence of an error. If the terms have NOT been accepted, do\n        NOT call `ProvisionFirebase` or implement Firebase: call\n        `show_aistudio_ui` with `ui_type: \"firebase_setup\"` again, STOP, and\n        wait for the user to respond. Only once the terms are accepted, you MUST\n        call `rpc_action` with `service_name: \"fax\"`, `method_name:\n        \"ProvisionFirebase\"` to provision the database. Arguments for\n        `ProvisionFirebase`:\n\n        *   `platform`: `web` or `android` (required)\n        *   `project_id`: the existing Firebase / Google Cloud project ID to\n            provision into. Required when the user named a project and on repeat\n            calls; see [Project Selection](#project-selection).\n        *   `firestore_region`: (optional)\n        *   `database_id`: (optional)\n\n        > [!CAUTION] You MUST call the `ProvisionFirebase` RPC (via\n        > `rpc_action`) after verifying terms acceptance on resume before\n        > performing any other task (such as writing integration code, modifying\n        > files, building, or linting). If you skip this, the database will not\n        > be provisioned and the application will fail.\n\n2.  **Deploy Firebase Rules**:\n\n    *   When you need to deploy rules (e.g., changes made to `firestore.rules`),\n        call `rpc_action` with `service_name: \"fax\"`, `method_name:\n        \"DeployRules\"`. Arguments: None.\n\n### Project Selection\n\n> [!IMPORTANT] **Honor the user\'s project.** If the user named a Firebase or\n> Google Cloud project ID anywhere in the conversation (for example \"use the\n> existing Firebase project `my-project-123`\"), you **MUST** pass it verbatim as\n> `project_id` on every `ProvisionFirebase` call. If you omit `project_id`, the\n> backend silently picks one of the user\'s other projects, and the app ends up\n> wired to a project the user did not choose. On the first call, omit\n> `project_id` only when the user has not named a project; repeat calls follow\n> the rules below.\n\n*   `project_id` must be the project **ID** (lowercase letters, digits, and\n    hyphens, as shown in the Firebase console URL), not a display name or\n    project number. The backend uses the value literally. If the user gave only\n    a display name or number, ask them for the project ID before calling\n    `ProvisionFirebase`.\n*   Do not tell the user that Firebase is being set up in a specific project\n    unless you are passing that project as `project_id`.\n*   On repeat `ProvisionFirebase` calls, pass the same `project_id` you used\n    originally. If the app was provisioned earlier without one, pass the\n    `projectId` already in `firebase-applet-config.json`, unless it is the remix\n    placeholder `remixed-project-id`.\n\nIf you passed `project_id`, confirm the returned `firebase_project_id` (or\n`firebaseProjectId`) and `projectId` in `firebase-applet-config.json` match it."
    num_lines: 842
    num_bytes: 38768
    is_skill_file: true
    skill_metadata {
      name: "firebase-integration-rpc"
      description: "Integrates Firebase (Firestore and Auth) into applications, generate secure rules, and handle errors.\n\nUse this skill whenever the application requires:\n* Persistent Data & Database: Storing, querying, and persisting application state, documents, or records across sessions and devices (even if Firebase is not explicitly named).\n* Multi-Role & Access Control (RBAC): Applications with distinct user roles or permissions (e.g., admins, super admins, managers, employees, assignees, creators, customers).\n* User Authentication & Accounts: App user sign-in/login, user profiles, credentials, or private user data.\n* Security Hardening & Rules: Designing schemas, writing/auditing `firestore.rules`, enforcing role validation, or debugging permission errors.\n* SDK & Tool Setup: Provisioning Firebase via `ProvisionFirebase` RPC, initializing the Firebase SDK in React/TypeScript, or deploying rules via `DeployRules` RPC.\n\nDo NOT use this skill for:\n* General GCP resource management outside of the specific Firebase tools provided.\n* Purely transient or single-session apps with no persistence, user accounts, or shared state (e.g., simple unit converters, local calculators).\n* Applications explicitly requesting a relational SQL database like PostgreSQL or Cloud SQL (use cloudsql-setup instead)."
    }
