    thinking: "**Prioritizing Category Visibility**\n\nI\'m currently focused on the initial user experience within explore reports. It seems crucial that the \"All Categories\" section, encompassing things like water saving and greener living, takes precedence. My thinking is to ensure this section is immediately visible, acting as the main point of entry, with other categories appearing subsequently. This seems the best way to do it.\n\n\n**Clarifying Initial Display**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on de-clustering the explore reports. The focus is to make the \"All Categories\" section, featuring water saving and greener living, the immediate focal point. This includes ensuring it isn\'t buried under search inputs or suggestions. My strategy is to highlight this section at the very top, acting as the primary entry point, with other categories appearing below. The All Categories hub, with its count badge, will be prominent.\n\n\n**Refining Tool Placement**\n\nI\'m now determining the optimal position for secondary tools, especially following the category section. I\'m considering a secondary row for the search bar (with an integrated scope picker), status filters (Submitted, Under Review, etc.), sort order options (Newest, Oldest), and a view mode switcher (Grid/List). A subtle quick suggestions row should be collapsible or unobtrusive. The scope bar (\"All Community Reports\" vs \"My Reports\") requires careful placement, considering its relationship to the main category bar or page heading.\n\n\n**Defining Category Order**\n\nI\'ve finalized the arrangement of the category bar. The top priority is a clear presentation of four key categories: Water Saving, Electricity Saving, Recycling, and Greener Living, each with distinct icons and color accents. These will be followed by other categories in a horizontally scrollable bar. Each category pill will feature an icon, a label (bilingual-friendly), and a report count. Selected states use a deep pine-green theme, while unselected states have a clean white card design with a subtle border. Below the category bar, I\'ll place a sleek, spacious secondary search bar.\n\n\n"
