  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing UI Structure**\n\nI\'m digging into the UI code of `ExploreReportsPage.tsx`, specifically lines 213-480, to understand its structure. My focus is on the user\'s initial experience and how the \"all categories\" aspect is presented. I need to get this clear. I\'m looking at how the elements are currently organized on the page and how to best deliver the \"all categories\" view.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Category Visibility**\n\nI\'m thinking the existing structure buried the category pills. The main thing, \"all categories\", got lost in a crowded interface. It was a single giant box containing search, dropdowns, and category pills, which were at the bottom separated by a thin line. I\'ll restructure to ensure \"all categories\" is the central, emphasized element. The user clearly wanted that. A clean header with a badge is my current focus.\n\n\n**Refining Hero Bar Design**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the \"Main Categories Hub\" design. I\'m prioritizing the hero bar, ensuring \"All Categories\" is the central, prominent button. I want it to be the first thing the user sees. I\'m focusing on the navigation card\'s elegant header row, including the icon, label, and reset options. The styling for the \"All Categories\" pill will be key, giving it a distinctive look with the \"Layers\" icon, a label, and a count badge. I\'m aiming for a deep forest green for selection, to give it confidence.\n\n\n**Designing Category Layout**\n\nI\'m now iterating on the visual presentation. The prioritized sustainability pillars will follow the central \"All Categories\" button. I\'ve chosen icons and colors: Water Saving (blue, Droplets), Electricity Saving (amber, Zap), Recycling (emerald, Recycle), and Greener Living (vibrant green, Leaf). These will be followed by other categories. I\'m focusing on a smooth, horizontal, touch-friendly scroll, aiming for a spacious feel.\n\n\n"
    tool_calls {
      id: "call_96283"
